<TopicPage slug="subjects/public-administration/foundational/the-administrative-state">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Public administration is the study of how government actually runs — the agencies, budgets and
    civil services that turn laws into permits, pensions and pavement. It lives in the gap between
    what politics promises and what organisations can deliver, and its core design problem is
    permanent: how do you make unelected power accountable?
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the machinery and its checks" widget="equationStep" config={adminCfg} />

  <h2>Woodrow Wilson's question, still open</h2>
  <p>
    The field's founding essay — Wilson's "The Study of Administration" (1887) — asked how
    government can be run competently without corrupting democracy. A century of answers:
    merit-based civil service to kill the spoils system, scientific management, then the New Public
    Management's markets and metrics, and today's renewed focus on equity and trust. Each wave
    rediscovers Weber's trade-off: rules buy fairness and cost flexibility.
  </p>

  <h2>Budgets are policy</h2>
  <p>
    Follow the money and administration becomes concrete: budget cycles, appropriations, line-item
    politics and audits. A programme without a budget line is a press release; an implementation
    without cost accounting is a scandal in waiting. Public budgeting is where policy intention is
    tested against arithmetic — which is why NASPAA makes it a core competency.
  </p>

  <h2>Implementation is where laws go to be tested</h2>
  <p>
    The classic finding of implementation research (Pressman &amp; Wildavsky): a policy's fate is
    decided by the hundreds of small decisions, consents and delays after the bill passes.
    Street-level bureaucrats — teachers, caseworkers, inspectors — exercise discretion that is
    policy in effect. Governance happens at the desk, not just the chamber.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASPAA Universal Required Competencies; Wilson, "The Study of Administration" (1887); Pressman &amp; Wildavsky, <em>Implementation</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
